# Question: for a year of work that needs three engineers, what does it cost
# to hire them, to bring in contractors or to outsource it to an agency, and
# how likely is hiring to be the cheapest option?
#
# Run it (it uses the shared formulas):
#   ubc --scenario team/in-house-vs-contractors
# Change an assumption, for example contractors from a cheaper market:
#   ubc --set 'day_rate = 300 to 500' --scenario team/in-house-vs-contractors
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens in_house --scenario team/in-house-vs-contractors
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. Amounts are in euros for one
# year. Only the money is compared: knowledge that stays in the company when
# you hire is not priced here.
#
# Sources: rules of thumb for the loaded cost (salary plus 25-40%) and
# typical experience for the rest; there is no published benchmark for these
# rates, so replace them with quotes from your own market.

# Assumptions
engineers = 3                     # people the work needs for a year; your own plan
billable_days = 210               # days a year a contractor or an agency engineer bills; typical experience
salary = 50k to 80k               # yearly gross salary of an engineer you hire; your payroll or local salary surveys
overhead = 1.25 to 1.4            # taxes, benefits and equipment on top of salary; rule of thumb 25-40%
hiring = 15k to 40k               # per hire: hiring and ramp-up; typical experience (see hiring-cost and onboarding)
day_rate = 400 to 700             # a freelance contractor's day rate; quotes from your market
contractor_ramp_up = 5k to 15k    # per contractor: work lost while they learn the codebase; typical experience
agency_rate = 450 to 900          # an agency's day rate per engineer, management included; agency quotes
coordination = 0.1 to 0.3         # extra share of the bill spent on your side: specs, reviews; typical experience

# Calculation
in_house = engineers * (loaded_cost(salary, overhead) + hiring)                 # salaries plus hiring
contractors = engineers * (day_rate * billable_days + contractor_ramp_up)       # rates plus ramp-up
outsourcing = engineers * agency_rate * billable_days * (1 + coordination)      # rates plus coordination

# Result
in_house
contractors
outsourcing
P(in_house < min(contractors, outsourcing))   # how likely is hiring to be the cheapest option?
